            <ArticleTitle>Catatonia in Patients with Dementia Admitted to a Geriatric Psychiatry Ward.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S103-S105</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/jnrp.jnrp_47_17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To determine the frequency and characteristics of catatonia in persons with dementia, all patients admitted to an old age psychiatry ward were screened for catatonia with the Bush-Francis Catatonia Screening Instrument (BFCSI) over a period of 6 months. Thirty-seven patients were admitted, and there were 14 patients with dementia. The rate of catatonia in these patients was 42.8% (6 out of 14). Five out of six of these patients received treatment for catatonia with lorazepam, all achieving complete remission. Catatonia occurred frequently in patients admitted with dementia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
